 112865, 10.1016\/j.jcp.2024.112865, 2024.","DOI":"10.1016\/j.jcp.2024.112865"},{"key":"ref60","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","unstructured":"Wang, Y., Lai, C.-Y., Prior, D.\u00a0J., and Cowen-Breen, C.: Deep learning the flow law of Antarctic ice shelves, Science, 387, 1219\u20131224, 10.1126\/science.adp3300, 2025.","DOI":"10.1126\/science.adp3300"},{"key":"ref61","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","unstructured":"Weertman, J.: On the Sliding of Glaciers, J. Glaciol., 3, 33\u201338, 10.3189\/S0022143000024709, 1957.","DOI":"10.3189\/S0022143000024709"}],"container-title":["The Cryosphere"],"language":"en","link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/tc.copernicus.org\/articles\/20\/3533\/2026\/tc-20-3533-2026.pdf","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"deposited":{"date-parts":[[2026,9,12]],"date-time":"2026-09-12T09:54:38Z","timestamp":1789206878000},"score":36.109005,"resource":{"primary":{"URL":"https:\/\/tc.copernicus.org\/articles\/20\/3533\/2026\/"}},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,19]]},"references-count":61,"journal-issue":{"issue":"6","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2026]]}},"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.5194\/tc-20-3533-2026","relation":{"has-preprint":[{"id-type":"doi","id":"10.5194\/egusphere-2025-3964","asserted-by":"subject"}],"has-review":[{"id-type":"doi","id":"10.5194\/egusphere-2025-3964-RC1","asserted-by":"subject"},{"id-type":"doi","id":"10.5194\/egusphere-2025-3964-AC1","asserted-by":"subject"},{"id-type":"doi","id":"10.5194\/egusphere-2025-3964-RC2","asserted-by":"subject"},{"id-type":"doi","id":"10.5194\/egusphere-2025-3964-AC2","asserted-by":"subject"},{"id-type":"doi","id":"10.5194\/egusphere-2025-3964-AC2","asserted-by":"object"},{"id-type":"doi","id":"10.5194\/egusphere-2025-3964-AC1","asserted-by":"object"},{"id-type":"doi","id":"10.5194\/egusphere-2025-3964-RC2","asserted-by":"object"},{"id-type":"doi","id":"10.5194\/egusphere-2025-3964-RC1","asserted-by":"object"}],"is-part-of":[{"id-type":"doi","id":"10.5067\/6B6B225B8V2D","asserted-by":"subject"},{"id-type":"doi","id":"10.5281\/zenodo.20182294","asserted-by":"subject"},{"id-type":"doi","id":"10.5281\/zenodo.14889235","asserted-by":"subject"},{"id-type":"doi","id":"10.5281\/zenodo.7850841","asserted-by":"subject"}]},"ISSN":["1994-0424"],"issn-type":[{"value":"1994-0424","type":"electronic"}],"published":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,19]]}},{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,19]],"date-time":"2026-06-19T05:53:34Z","timestamp":1781848414206,"version":"3.54.5"},"posted":{"date-parts":[[2025,9,22]]},"group-title":"Ice sheets\/Greenland","reference-count":0,"publisher":"Copernicus GmbH","license":[{"start":{"date-parts":[[2025,9,22]],"date-time":"2025-09-22T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1758499200000},"content-version":"vor","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"https:\/\/creativecommons.org\/licenses\/by\/4.0\/"}],"funder":[{"DOI":"10.13039\/100000105","name":"Office of Advanced Cyberinfrastructure","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","award":["2118285"],"award-info":[{"award-number":["2118285"]}],"id":[{"id":"10.13039\/100000105","id-type":"DOI","asserted-by":"publisher"}]},{"DOI":"10.13039\/501100009708","name":"Novo Nordisk Fonden","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","award":["NNF23OC00807040"],"award-info":[{"award-number":["NNF23OC00807040"]}],"id":[{"id":"10.13039\/501100009708","id-type":"DOI","asserted-by":"publisher"}]}],"content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"abstract":"<jats:p>Abstract. Subglacial topography beneath the Greenland Ice Sheet is a fundamental control on its dynamics and response to changes in the climate system. Yet, it remains challenging to measure directly, and existing representations of the subglacial topography rely on a limited number of observations. Although the use of mass conservation and the development of BedMachine Greenland substantially improved the representation of the bed topography, this approach is limited to fast-flowing sectors and is less effective in regions with complex, alpine topography. As an alternative to traditional numerical methods, recent work has explored using Physics Informed Neural Networks (PINNs), constrained by only one physical law, to solve forward and inverse problems in ice sheet modeling. Building on this work, we assess three PINN frameworks constrained by distinct conservation laws, showing that PINNs informed with a single conservation law are not sufficient for regions with sparse measurements and complex topographies. To that end, we introduce a novel approach that involves coupling two conservation laws within a PINN framework to infer the subglacial topography and test this approach for three regions with distinct environments in Greenland. This PINN is trained with both the conservation of mass and an approximation of the conservation of momentum (the Shelfy-Stream Approximation), which allows us to simultaneously infer the ice thickness and basal shear stress using observations of ice velocities, surface elevation, and the apparent mass balance in a mixed inversion problem. We compare the predicted ice thickness to ground-truth ice-penetrating radar measurements of ice thickness, showing that the PINN informed with two conservation laws is capable of inferring ice thickness in sparsely surveyed regions. Furthermore, comparisons of predicted bed topographies with BedMachine Greenland show that this approach is capable of discovering new bed features in slower-moving regions and in regions of complex topography, highlighting its potential for better constraining the bed topography of the Greenland Ice Sheet.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.5194\/egusphere-2025-3964","type":"posted-content","created":{"date-parts":[[2025,9,22]],"date-time":"2025-09-22T13:44:53Z","timestamp":1758548693000},"source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":0,"title":["Inferring subglacial topography using physics informed machine learning constrained by two conservation laws"],"prefix":"10.5194","author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0002-1350-0680","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Mansa","family":"Krishna","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-9171-6714","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Gong","family":"Cheng","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0000-0001-5219-1310","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Mathieu","family":"Morlighem","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"member":"3145","deposited":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,19]],"date-time":"2026-06-19T05:35:23Z","timestamp":1781847323000},"score":39.629723,"resource":{"primary":{"URL":"https:\/\/egusphere.copernicus.org\/preprints\/2025\/egusphere-2025-3964\/"}},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,9,22]]},"references-count":0,"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.5194\/egusphere-2025-3964","relation":{"has-comment":[{"id-type":"doi","id":"10.5194\/egusphere-2025-3964-AC1","asserted-by":"subject"},{"id-type":"doi","id":"10.5194\/egusphere-2025-3964-AC2","asserted-by":"subject"}],"has-review":[{"id-type":"doi","id":"10.5194\/egusphere-2025-3964-RC1","asserted-by":"subject"},{"id-type":"doi","id":"10.5194\/egusphere-2025-3964-RC2","asserted-by":"subject"},{"id-type":"doi","id":"10.5194\/egusphere-2025-3964-RC1","asserted-by":"object"},{"id-type":"doi","id":"10.5194\/egusphere-2025-3964-RC2","asserted-by":"object"},{"id-type":"doi","id":"10.5194\/egusphere-2025-3964-AC2","asserted-by":"object"},{"id-type":"doi","id":"10.5194\/egusphere-2025-3964-AC1","asserted-by":"object"}],"is-preprint-of":[{"id-type":"doi","id":"10.5194\/tc-20-3533-2026","asserted-by":"subject"},{"id-type":"doi","id":"10.5194\/tc-20-3533-2026","asserted-by":"object"}]},"published":{"date-parts":[[2025,9,22]]},"subtype":"preprint"},{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2024,3,19]],"date-time":"2024-03-19T00:28:53Z","timestamp":1710808133414},"posted":{"date-parts":[[2024]]},"group-title":"SSRN","reference-count":0,"publisher":"Elsevier BV","content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"DOI":"10.2139\/ssrn.4763750","type":"posted-content","created":{"date-parts":[[2024,3,18]],"date-time":"2024-03-18T18:17:55Z","timestamp":1710785875000},"source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":0,"title":["Discovering Artificial Viscosity Models for Discontinuous Galerkin Approximation of Conservation Laws Using Physics-Informed Machine Learning"],"prefix":"10.2139","author":[{"given":"Matteo","family":"Caldana","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Paola  F.","family":"Antonietti","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Luca","family":"Dede&apos;","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"member":"78","deposited":{"date-parts":[[2024,3,18]],"date-time":"2024-03-18T18:17:55Z","timestamp":1710785875000},"score":38.08285,"resource":{"primary":{"URL":"https:\/\/www.ssrn.com\/abstract=4763750"}},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2024]]},"references-count":0,"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.2139\/ssrn.4763750","published":{"date-parts":[[2024]]},"subtype":"preprint"},{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,7,7]],"date-time":"2026-07-07T20:31:14Z","timestamp":1783456274047,"version":"3.55.0"},"posted":{"date-parts":[[2025,11,3]]},"reference-count":0,"publisher":"Wiley","license":[{"start":{"date-parts":[[2025,11,3]],"date-time":"2025-11-03T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1762128000000},"content-version":"unspecified","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"https:\/\/creativecommons.org\/licenses\/by\/4.0\/"}],"content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"abstract":"<jats:p>Physics-Informed Machine Learning (PIML) represents a paradigm shift in computational modeling by integrating physical laws and constraints directly into machine learning frameworks. This approach addresses fundamental limitations of traditional data-driven methods, which often fail to maintain physical consistency and struggle with sparse, noisy data in high-dimensional systems. In this paper, we provide a comprehensive overview of PIML, covering its theoretical foundations, mathematical formulations, and key methodologies including Physics-Informed Neural Networks (PINNs) and neural operators. We demonstrate how PIML techniques can be applied across diverse domains including fluid dynamics, quantum computing, and environmental modeling. Through detailed case studies and performance evaluations, we illustrate the superior accuracy and data efficiency of PIML approaches compared to conventional methods. Our work serves as a comprehensive reference for researchers and practitioners seeking to leverage the synergy between machine learning and physical modeling.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.22541\/au.175915664.45104382\/v2","type":"posted-content","created":{"date-parts":[[2025,11,3]],"date-time":"2025-11-03T20:33:48Z","timestamp":1762202028000},"source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":1,"title":["Physics-Informed Machine Learning: Bridging Data-Driven Models and Physical Laws"],"prefix":"10.22541","author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0001-1956-5824","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Mohamed","family":"Salem","sequence":"first","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0007-7470-5870","authenticated-orcid":false,"given":"Mostafa","family":"Youssef","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"member":"311","link":[{"URL":"https:\/\/www.authorea.com\/doi\/pdf\/10.22541\/au.175915664.45104382\/v2","content-type":"unspecified","content-version":"vor","intended-application":"similarity-checking"}],"deposited":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,30]],"date-time":"2026-06-30T22:10:56Z","timestamp":1782857456000},"score":36.208477,"resource":{"primary":{"URL":"https:\/\/www.authorea.com\/doi\/full\/10.22541\/au.175915664.45104382\/v2"}},"issued":{"date-parts":[[2025,11,3]]},"references-count":0,"URL":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.22541\/au.175915664.45104382\/v2","published":{"date-parts":[[2025,11,3]]},"subtype":"preprint"},{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,30]],"date-time":"2026-06-30T22:53:52Z","timestamp":1782860032127,"version":"3.54.5"},"posted":{"date-parts":[[2025,9,29]]},"reference-count":0,"publisher":"Wiley","content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"abstract":"<jats:p>Physics-Informed Machine Learning (PIML) represents a paradigm shift in computational modeling by integrating physical laws and constraints directly into machine learning frameworks. This approach addresses fundamental limitations of traditional data-driven methods, which often fail to maintain physical consistency and struggle with sparse, noisy data in high-dimensional systems. In this paper, we provide a comprehensive overview of PIML, covering its theoretical foundations, mathematical formulations, and key methodologies including Physics-Informed Neural Networks (PINNs) and neural operators. We demonstrate how PIML techniques can be applied across diverse domains including fluid dynamics, quantum computing, and environmental modeling. Through detailed case studies and performance evaluations, we illustrate the superior accuracy and data efficiency of PIML approaches compared to conventional methods. Our work serves as a comprehensive reference for researchers and practitioners seeking to leverage the synergy between machine learning and physical modeling.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.22541\/au.175915664.45104382\/v1","type":"posted-content","created":{"date-parts":[[2025,9,29]],"date-time":"2025-09-29T14:37:32Z","timestamp":1759156652000},"source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":0,"title":["Physics-Informed Machine Learning: Bridging Data-Driven Models and Physical Laws"],"prefix":"10.22541","author":[{"ORCID":"https:\/\/orcid.org\/0009-0001-1956-5824","authenticated-o